  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA low pressure system approaching the region today will be the\nculprit in producing showers and storms this weekend with heavy\nrainfall and strong to severe storm potential both on the table.\nShowers will start scattered on Saturday with the main,\nvertically stacked surface low remaining well west along the \nMississippi River. Some mid-level vorticity advection will help\nsupport some occasional showers today ahead of the low but\nrainfall should be relatively light today for those that see\nanything. The main show will start tonight as the low advances\neast and additional moisture enters the region. Forecast\nsoundings across northern Ohio show PW values over 2 inches\ntonight, which means that any rain that does occur will be\nheavy, efficient rain. Any rain that develops will be moving\nsouth to north with some speed but if recurring rain trains over\nthe same areas, then there will be flooding concerns tonight,\nmainly in Northwest Ohio.\n\nThe trends for Sunday for heavy rainfall and strong to severe\nstorms is going up with this forecast package. The low will pass\nto the south of the forecast area on Sunday. However, a\ndeformation band of precipitation will be on the north side of\nthis low, which will be well supported by the left exit region\nof one upper level jet and the right entrance region of another\nupper level jet. This broader support should allow for\nwidespread coverage of rain across the region on Sunday with\nefficient rainfall rates and have a higher QPF forecast to\nreflect these concerns. It would not be surprising for most \nfolks to get at least one inch of rain this weekend but \nrecurring rain episodes for any location would open up the \npotential for multiple inches of rain and an increased risk for \nFlash Flooding. On the severe threat, the low pressure system \nand upper level jet support are indicative of a decently sheared\nenvironment that could allow for strong to severe storms to \ndevelop. The main concern for severe storms will be the \ninstability. Rain and clouds across the region may limit the \noverall instability in the region and in turn the severe \npotential. However, there are hints that there could be a break \nin shower activity on Sunday morning, especially for the eastern\nhalf of the forecast area. If this break does materialize, then\nthe severe potential for Sunday will continue to trend upward. \nThe main storm concerns for Sunday will be strong to damaging \nwind gusts and even a low end concern for a tornado.\n\nAs of this morning, the area is delineated in a WPC Slight Risk\nfor Excessive Rainfall for today and Sunday and an SPC Marginal\nRisk for Severe Weather for Sunday. There is potential for a\nFlood Watch to be needed across the area, depending on how the\nrain evolves today/tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe upper trough that will move through the region this weekend\nwill amplify Sunday night into Monday and eventually form an\nupper level cut off low over the Tennessee Valley. This feature\nwill meander over the Ohio Valley next week and have enough lift\nto allow for daily rain chances on Monday and Tuesday. This low\nwill get absorbed by an upper trough entering the region on\nWednesday. This upper trough will support a surface trough\nacross the region, which should allow for additional rain\nchances on Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday. None of the days\nnext week will be a washout, but there will be scattered rain on\nthe scope that could allow for a brief impact. Temperatures\nshould be near normal in the 80s, perhaps slightly cooler if\nthere is more rain/cloud activity on a given day.",
